  "preamble": "Velar palatalization of [k] to the postalveolar affricate [tʃ] is a widespread historical phonetic shift, typically triggered by following front vowels or the palatal glide [j]. This process, often involving an intermediate palatal stop stage [c], is a defining feature of the Slavic First Palatalization and the development of several Romance languages from Latin. In English, this is seen in the 'ch' of words like 'chin' (from Proto-Germanic *kinnus). In Dravidian, Proto-Dravidian *k became c before front vowels in Tamil, Malayalam and Telugu (*kewi 'ear' > Tamil and Telugu cevi), while Kannada and Tulu keep k (kivi, kebi). In Faroese the lenis velar /k/, which continues Old Norse g, is realized as [t͡ʃ] before front vowels and /j/ (geva [t͡ʃeːva] 'to give').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Affrication, Velar Fronting, Yod-coalescence",
